nss_hal: park the core before copying the firmware over it

A warm reboot does not reset the NSS block: the previous kernel's
firmware keeps executing out of the load region, and
nss_hal_firmware_load() copies the new image straight over it. The
new firmware then comes up dead (no first interrupt) or mute (boot
interrupt arrives - possibly latched from the old session - but the
log ring stays empty and every phys_if message times out). Measured
on IPQ5018/GL-B3000: every warm reboot fails this way, every cold
boot works, and a u-boot tftpboot before the kernel also "fixes" it
because reinitializing the GMAC crashes the old firmware first.

Assert the core-local reset clamp before the copy, exactly like
nss_hal_remove() already does for the rmmod path (same bug, same
mechanism - that path panicked instead). core_reset() releases the
clamp in its normal boot sequence. The GCC block resets are left
alone: on IPQ5018 they gate hardware shared with the host GMAC and
asserting them kills ethernet past the point a hard reset helps.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/nss_hal/nss_hal.c
+++ b/nss_hal/nss_hal.c
@@ -83,6 +83,22 @@ int nss_hal_firmware_load(struct nss_ctx
 		return rc;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A warm reboot does not reset the NSS block, so the previous
+	 * kernel's firmware is still executing out of this very region.
+	 * Park the core behind its local reset clamp before replacing its
+	 * instruction memory - core_reset() releases the clamp in its usual
+	 * sequence later. Without this the copy races the running core and
+	 * the new image comes up dead or mute on every warm reboot, while
+	 * cold boots (core held in reset by hardware) are fine - measured
+	 * on IPQ5018. The clamp is core-local; the GCC block resets must
+	 * NOT be touched here, on IPQ5018 they gate hardware shared with
+	 * the host GMAC and asserting them kills ethernet beyond even a
+	 * hard reset's help.
+	 */
+	if (npd->nmap)
+		nss_write_32(npd->nmap, NSS_REGS_RESET_CTRL_OFFSET, 0x1);
+
 	nss_info("nss_driver - fw of size %d  bytes copied to load addr: %x, nss_id : %d\n", (int)nss_fw->size, npd->load_addr, nss_dev->id);
 	memcpy_toio(load_mem, nss_fw->data, nss_fw->size);
 	release_firmware(nss_fw);
